$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/ai-foundations/ai-rules-of-use

::ai-rules-of-use o1 - Sort before you paste::You want an assistant's help with a failing report script. Which item belongs in the redact first lane? {
=A stack trace that is useful for the bug but shows one customer's email address #Correct. Useful material with a personal detail inside is redacted first, then shared.
~A function from course practice code that your program permits you to share with its approved tool #Not quite. Permitted material with nothing sensitive inside goes in the share lane as it is; nothing needs redacting.
~A spreadsheet export of all customer records #Not quite. Customer records are personal as a whole, so they are never shared, not even redacted.
~Your private SSH key file, the one without .pub #Not quite. A private key is secret as a whole, so it is never shared.
}

::ai-rules-of-use o2 - Redact, keep the useful part::Your log shows this line. login failed for maria.lopez@example.test using key FAKE-KEY-7781, error 401 in auth.py line 42. Which version is safe and still helpful to paste? {
=login failed for [EMAIL] using key [API_KEY], error 401 in auth.py line 42 #Correct. The email and key are replaced with placeholders, while the error code, file and line number that help with the bug are kept.
~login failed for maria.lopez@example.test using key FAKE-KEY-77**, error 401 in auth.py line 42 #Not quite. Masking part of the key still leaks part of it, and the email address is untouched.
~login failed, please help #Not quite. This removes the secret but also throws away the error code, file and line number that would help.
~The full line as it is, then delete the chat afterwards #Not quite. Treat anything you paste as having left your control; deleting the chat later does not undo sharing it.
}
